{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=7.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =7 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =7{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =7{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 7{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce98d1f36ea22fc85e5889cab20bb9f5d1f93c570e96e4e7b5b69647724e675"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-7x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{7x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "7{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "7{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337375" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=2x-5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{x})}^{′}-(u{e}^{x}) & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-u{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{u{e}^{x}}-\\cancel{u{e}^{x}} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ce98d1f36ea22fc85e5889cab20bb9f5d1f93c570e96e4e7b5b69647724e675"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{5x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{-x}(5-2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-x}\\int {e}^{-x}(5-2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}\\int {e}^{x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337395" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{6x})}^{′}-6(u{e}^{6x}) & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+6u{e}^{6x}-6u{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{6x}} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057172/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd79d6b321a85fbbec02854dabf978c76ed12a3f86405977fc73403e677555bc"} {"question" "q-337533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe